<h1>Metal Detectors &amp; Inspection Systems</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Conveyor-mounted inspection systems scan every product on the packaging line for contaminants, weight deviations, label defects, and package integrity failures, then trigger <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject mechanisms</a> to remove non-conforming product before it reaches the consumer. The four primary inspection technologies are metal detectors, X-ray systems, checkweighers, and vision inspection. For food, FDA treats hard or sharp foreign objects 7 mm to 25 mm long in ready-to-eat food as an adulteration hazard (see <a href="https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/cpg-sec-555425-foods-adulteration-involving-hard-or-sharp-foreign-objects" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FDA CPG Sec. 555.425</a>), which is why contaminant detection is a common critical control point.</p>
<h2 id="metal-heading">Conveyor Metal Detectors</h2>
<p>Conveyor metal detectors pass product through a balanced coil aperture that generates an electromagnetic field. Metal contamination, ferrous, non-ferrous, or stainless steel, disturbs the field, triggering a detection signal. The detector sends a reject command to the downstream reject mechanism after calculating the delay for product travel from the aperture to the reject point.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/inspection-fortress-stealth-metal-detector.webp" width="1000" height="625" alt="A metal detector head with a touchscreen mounted over a short white belt conveyor on a wheeled stainless steel frame." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A Fortress Technology Stealth metal detector on a conveyor. The belt carries product through the rectangular aperture, where the coils sense metal. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://fortresstechnology.com/solutions/stealth-metal-detector/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fortress Technology</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Detection sensitivity depends on aperture size, product effect (the electromagnetic signal the product itself generates), and contaminant type. The <a href="https://www.mt.com/mt_ext_files/Editorial/Generic/5/PI-Safeline-Metal-Detection-Guide_Editorial-Generic_1190206474429_files/MetalDetectionGuide_Preview_1190206474429.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mettler-Toledo Safeline Metal Detection Guide</a> states that ferrous metal is the easiest to detect, while non-magnetic stainless grades such as 304 and 316 are often the most difficult, with non-ferrous metals usually in between. The smallest detectable sphere for each metal depends on aperture height, product, and line speed, so ask the supplier to test your actual product. Wet, salty, or conductive products (fresh meat, cheese, salty snacks) generate high product effect that masks small contaminant signals, requiring advanced multi-frequency or multi-simultaneous-frequency (MSF) detectors.</p>
<p>Multi-frequency detectors used on <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food packaging lines</a> run or switch between several frequencies to separate the product signal from the metal signal. This reduces false rejects on high-product-effect foods while keeping detection performance.</p>
<p>Metal detector conveyor integration requires a metal-free zone around the aperture, no metal conveyor components within the aperture area. The conveyor belt uses non-metallic splices (sewn or plastic pin). The conveyor frame transitions from steel to plastic or stainless steel through the detection zone. The Mettler-Toledo guide gives typical metal-free zone values of 1.5 times aperture height for stationary metal and 2.0 times aperture height for moving metal, and notes the manufacturer's installation instructions set the actual figure. Most inspection manufacturers supply the conveyor section with the detector for this reason.</p>
<h2 id="xray-heading">X-Ray Inspection Systems</h2>
<p>X-ray inspection systems use low-energy X-ray beams to create a density image of every product on the conveyor. The X-ray image reveals contaminants that differ in density from the product, metal, glass, stone, bone, ceramic, dense plastics, and rubber. Software analyzes each image in real time and triggers rejection when a contaminant is detected.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/inspection-eagle-pack-400-hc-xray.webp" width="845" height="704" alt="Front view of a stainless steel x-ray inspection cabinet on legs with a touchscreen, a signal light stack, and a conveyor tunnel on each side." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>An Eagle Pack 400 HC x-ray inspection system for packaged food. The conveyor carries product through the enclosed cabinet, where the x-ray image is taken. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.eaglepi.com/products/x-ray-machines/pack-400-hc/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Eagle Product Inspection</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<p>X-ray systems detect contaminant types that metal detectors miss. Glass fragments in glass jars, stone chips in grain products, bone fragments in meat, and rubber gasket pieces in sauced products are invisible to metal detectors but visible on X-ray. X-ray systems also perform secondary inspection functions, fill level verification, missing component detection (e.g., a missing prize in a cereal box), mass estimation, and seal integrity checking.</p>
<p>Systems range from compact single-beam units for individual product inspection to dual-beam and side-shoot configurations for detecting contaminants in tall or irregularly shaped products.</p>
<p>In the US, conveyor X-ray inspectors are cabinet X-ray systems regulated by <a href="https://www.fda.gov/radiation-emitting-products/security-systems/cabinet-x-ray-systems-closed-x-ray-systems" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FDA</a> under <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/21/1020.40" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR 1020.40</a>. The standard limits emission to 0.5 milliroentgen in one hour at any point 5 cm outside the cabinet and requires at least two safety interlocks per door, "X-RAY ON" indicators, and warning labels. Many states also require registration of X-ray equipment, so involve the plant's radiation safety contact before installation.</p>
<p>X-ray systems cost significantly more than metal detectors because of the X-ray generator, detector array, shielding, and image software. The higher cost is justified when the contamination risk includes non-metallic foreign objects or when secondary inspection functions (fill level, component counting) replace separate inspection stations. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for inspection system cost factors.</p>
<h2 id="checkweigh-heading">In-Motion Checkweighers</h2>
<p>Checkweighers weigh every product in motion on the conveyor without stopping the line. A precision load cell under a short conveyor section captures the product weight as it crosses the weigh zone. Products outside the target weight range (underweight or overweight) trigger a reject signal. Checkweighers verify fill accuracy, detect missing components, and support net contents compliance. In the US, inspectors test packages using <a href="https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/nist-handbook-133-current-edition" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIST Handbook 133</a>, Checking the Net Contents of Packaged Goods.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/inspection-fortress-raptor-checkweigher.webp" width="1000" height="625" alt="A stainless steel checkweigher with a touchscreen on a central column, a blue belt under a clear cover, and a perforated side cabinet." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A Fortress Technology Raptor checkweigher. Product is weighed in motion as it crosses the belt under the clear cover. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://fortresstechnology.com/solutions/raptor-checkweigher/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fortress Technology</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Checkweigher accuracy depends on product speed, vibration isolation, and weigh zone length. Higher speeds reduce weighing time per product, which reduces accuracy. The weigh conveyor must be long enough for the product to be fully on the weigh section and settle before the reading is taken, so it is sized from the longest product and belt speed. Ask suppliers for accuracy at your product weight and rate, stated as a standard deviation. Products must arrive with controlled spacing, only one product on the weigh section at a time. An <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing conveyor</a> or speed-up belt upstream creates the required gap.</p>
<p>Checkweighers are sold with integrated infeed, weigh, and reject conveyors. Achievable rate falls as product length grows, because each product must cross the weigh belt alone.</p>
<p>Checkweighers integrate with upstream filling equipment through feedback control. The checkweigher's statistical weight data feeds back to the filler's PLC, allowing automatic fill weight adjustment to maintain target weight and minimize overfill giveaway. A filler that runs consistently heavy gives away product on every package, and checkweigher feedback reduces that giveaway.</p>
<h2 id="vision-heading">Vision Inspection Systems</h2>
<p>Vision inspection systems use industrial cameras and image processing software to inspect product appearance, label accuracy, package integrity, and dimensional conformance at line speed. A camera captures an image of each product as it passes a fixed position on the conveyor. The software analyzes the image against trained criteria and sends a pass/fail signal to the reject controller before the product reaches the reject point.</p>
<p>Common vision inspection checks on packaging conveyors include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Label presence and position:</strong> Confirms a label exists and is positioned within tolerance. Detects missing labels, skewed labels, and wrinkled labels.</li>
<li><strong>Label content verification:</strong> OCR reads text on labels (lot codes, date codes, ingredient lists) and verifies correctness. Catches wrong-product labels and expired date codes.</li>
<li><strong>Barcode and 2D code readability:</strong> Scans barcodes and QR codes to verify readability and correct data. Unreadable codes cause retailer chargebacks and shipping rejections.</li>
<li><strong>Cap and seal integrity:</strong> Detects missing caps, crooked caps, broken tamper bands, and incomplete heat seals. Critical for product safety and shelf life.</li>
<li><strong>Fill level:</strong> Measures visible fill level through transparent or translucent containers. Detects underfilled and overfilled packages.</li>
<li><strong>Color and surface defects:</strong> Detects discoloration, surface damage, foreign material on the product surface, and cosmetic defects that indicate quality problems.</li>
</ul>
<p>Smart cameras integrate the camera, processor, and I/O in a single unit, simplifying installation on existing conveyors.</p>
<p>Vision system integration requires controlled lighting (LED bar lights, dome lights, or backlight panels), a fixed camera mount position relative to the conveyor, and a product trigger sensor that tells the camera when to capture. Product tracking from the camera position to the <a href="/conveyor-functions/pusher-diverter/">reject mechanism</a> uses an encoder on the conveyor to calculate when the defective product reaches the reject point.</p>
<h2 id="placement-heading">Where to Place Inspection Systems</h2>
<p>Inspection system placement follows two principles: <strong>inspect as early as possible</strong> (to prevent defective product from wasting downstream processing) and <strong>inspect as late as possible</strong> (to catch contamination introduced by downstream equipment). Best practice uses both, early inspection catches incoming defects, final inspection catches everything.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>After filling, before sealing:</strong> Checkweigher verifies fill weight. Rejects underweight and overweight before the package is sealed, allowing product rework (refill underweight, reduce overfill).</li>
<li><strong>After labeling:</strong> Vision system verifies label presence, position, content, and barcode readability. Rejects mislabeled product before case packing.</li>
<li><strong>Before case packing:</strong> Metal detector scans individual products at maximum sensitivity (small aperture). Individual product inspection detects smaller contaminants than case-level inspection.</li>
<li><strong>After case packing (final inspection):</strong> X-ray or metal detector scans the sealed case as the last check before palletizing. Catches contamination introduced during case packing. Some retailers require this final inspection point specifically.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every inspection point requires a <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject system</a> immediately downstream. The conveyor design at each inspection station must provide controlled product spacing, consistent belt speed, and adequate clearance for the reject mechanism. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation conveyors</a> upstream of inspection stations ensure steady product flow for reliable detection.</p>
<h2 id="conveyor-heading">Conveyor Requirements</h2>
<p>Inspection systems impose specific requirements on the conveyor sections that carry product through the inspection zone:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Consistent speed:</strong> VFD-controlled motors with encoder feedback hold belt speed steady. Speed variation causes checkweigher inaccuracy and reject timing errors. The conveyor speed signal feeds the inspection system's product tracking calculation.</li>
<li><strong>Product spacing:</strong> Checkweighers require one product on the weigh cell at a time. Metal detectors and X-ray systems need sufficient gap between products to assign reject signals to individual products. Minimum spacing depends on the inspection system and reject type, so get it from the supplier.</li>
<li><strong>Vibration isolation:</strong> Checkweighers require vibration-free mounting. The weigh conveyor section isolates from the main conveyor frame using rubber mounts or spring isolators. Adjacent equipment vibration (compressors, other conveyors) transmits through the floor and degrades weighing accuracy.</li>
<li><strong>Metal-free zones:</strong> Metal detector conveyor sections eliminate metal from the detection zone, non-metallic belt splices, plastic idler rollers, and stainless steel or plastic frame components within the aperture. The metal-free zone is set by aperture height (Mettler-Toledo cites 1.5 to 2.0 times aperture height as typical) and the manufacturer's instructions.</li>
<li><strong>Sanitary construction:</strong> <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Food-grade inspection conveyors</a> use stainless steel frames, FDA-compliant belts, IP69K motors, and open-frame designs for washdown cleaning. The inspection equipment itself must match the environmental rating.</li>
</ul>
