<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What inspection systems are used on packaging conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Four primary systems: metal detectors (ferrous/non-ferrous/SS contamination), X-ray (metal, glass, stone, bone), checkweighers (weight verification), and vision (labels, seals, codes, cosmetic). Most packaging lines deploy two or more at different inspection points.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">Metal detector vs. X-ray, what is the difference?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Metal detectors find metallic contamination only using electromagnetic fields. X-ray detects any dense foreign object, metal, glass, stone, bone, ceramic. Metal detectors cost less, often find smaller metal fragments in dry unpackaged product, and need no radiation safety program. X-ray costs more but finds non-metallic contaminants, can inspect product in foil or metallized film, and performs secondary checks (fill level, component counting).</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">Where should inspection systems go on the line?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Checkweighers after filling. Vision after labeling. Metal detectors before case packing (individual product) and after case packing (final check). X-ray as the final inspection point before palletizing. Each inspection point needs a downstream <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject mechanism</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">Do inspection systems need reject mechanisms?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Yes. Every inspection system detecting defects must have a reject mechanism to remove non-conforming product. A detector without a reject provides no safety benefit. Most manufacturers supply integrated conveyor-reject-inspection systems with reject confirmation sensors to verify successful removal.</p>

</div>
</div>
